out security as  important as these topics are it was of  course about citizen's right and the  same question came back time and again  with different personal twists one came  from a young man who came from Benelux  living in the UK who was of course  worried about his future another came  from a British lady who has an Italian  husband who was worried about his status  what's gonna happen with him and while  the questions may have been subtle  different the reasons for asking them  were always the same people want  certainty about their lives and their  future and certainty about the future of  the loved ones and I'm speaking here  about 3.5 million citizens in the UK who  came from a European Union member states  and about run round about 1 million  British people living elsewhere in the  European Union now these are large  numbers  but behind each of these numbers are  people with their very own personal  stories some are citizens from another  European Union country but they are born  in the United Kingdom others have  arrived later many have married have  children I've gone to university have  started their careers and they work in  the UK and respectively in the European  Union so they teach children they treat  patients they care for the elderly they  work in farms or factories they have  started their own business and provide a  living to many more so whatever their  own personal story is they all have one  thing in common and that is they want to  have peace of mind they want to have a  plan for their futures and the future of  their families and all they want to have  is to feel at home in the place they  call home  therefore honourable members protecting  these citizens right has always been our  top priority from the moment the  referendum result came clear thanks to  the excellent work of Michel Barney and  his negotiation team the withdrawal  agreement provides comprehensive  guarantees for these citizens it gives  them the protection and the certainty  they need it allows them to stay where  they live today under the same  conditions for as long as they want and  without any discrimination based on  their nationality this with rural  agreement and with what we have agreed  on puts of course clear obligations on  the United Kingdom and on the European  Union to safeguard these rights one  thing is to put them on paper and have a  thing is to implement them so once the  house has ratified the withdrawal  agreement our top priority will be to  implement our shared obligations both in  letter and in spirit and for me this is  a legal issue with a moral imperative  good news is that the United Kingdom has  already begun implementing the European  Union's settlement scheme to date about  2.5 million European citizens living in  the United Kingdom have already applied  for resident status most of these people  have now received proof of their right  to remain this is good news but you also  know there are cases some have not and  many more are yet to apply and there are  a number of concerns that have always  been raised there are the famous cases  of the French bakers and the Polish  chefs but shine a light on some of the  issues but seriously there are many  other cases where people are confused or  unable to apply for whatever reasons or  others have received requests for more  proof despite having lived in the  country for 40 years or more so we are  close in contact with the citizens and  the UK authorities on these issues we  need to avoid any discrimination or  added complexity during the application  process we need to ensure that all  citizens especially the most vulnerable  are fully supported throughout the  process and we need the guarantee that  the independent monitoring authority can  act in full independence to deal with  complaints this is of utmost importance  from our side the Commission will keep a  close and vigilant eye on the  implementation we're ready to help  European Union citizens living in the  United Kingdom to ensure they are  properly informed and supported and of  course we will do the same when it comes  to British citizens living in the  European Union all these questions I've  been speaking about I've raised with the  Prime Minister Johnson last week when I  visited London we will closely cooperate  with the United Kingdom to make sure  that we can get this right and that we  can solve any problems as  swiftly as possible finally honorable  members the good news is that there will  be continuity for the European Union  citizens living in the United Kingdom  and vice-versa for the British citizens  living in the European Union at the end  of the transition period but after the  end of the transition period the United  Kingdom will be a third country and  brexit will mean changes to those who  will want to make their future life on  either side of the channel we will have  to negotiate a new way forward and we're  getting ready to formulate the mandate  for those negotiations ahead of the 1st  of February we have as we have done in  the past we will in these negotiations  also make the citizens rights our main  priority and we will always negotiate in  good faith to build a new comprehensive  partnership with our British friends as  I've said to those students in London  it's the good old story about old  friends and new beginnings thank you  [Applause]
 

